==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|ang|mear}}, {{l|ang|mearg}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{recons|marhaz|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{recons|márkos||horse|lang=ine-pro}}. Cognate with Old Frisian {{term|mar||horse|lang=ofs}}, {{term|merie||mare|lang=ofs}} (West Frisian {{term|merje||mare|lang=fy}}), Old Saxon ''[[merge#Old Saxon|merge]]'', {{term|meriha||mare|lang=osx}}, Dutch {{term|merrie||mare|lang=nl}}, Old High German {{term|marah||horse|lang=goh}}, {{term|meriha||mare|lang=goh}} (German {{term|Mähre||mare|lang=de}}), Old Norse {{term|marr||horse|lang=non}} (Icelandic {{term|mar|lang=is}}), {{term|merr||mare|lang=non}} (Swedish {{term|märr|lang=sv}}).  The Indo-European root is also the source of Irish ''[[marc#Irish|marc]]'' and Welsh ''[[march#Welsh|march]]'' ("horse").

